A||||5||Adrift||Applying Primer with a paint brush||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||09-12-2001||11:49 AM||||I was told that you can get great results when you use a paint brush to apply a thick coat of primer and then hand sand the whole car down - is this true? Also, whats the difference between automotive primer and the primers you can buy at home depots?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||09-12-2001||12:11 PM||||I've never heard that, and I wouldn't do it.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/halfmast_glory.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||09-12-2001||04:50 PM||||Did the person have a great big smile and smooth looking wink when they told you that?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick86GT||09-12-2001||06:20 PM||||EVERY flaw in the primer telegraphs into the finish paint....to sand enuff of the brush primered surface off to get it smooth (remove brush lines and overlap marks), you'd probably remove most of the primer<P>use only automotive primer/paint.  it is a vastly different and more refined product than house paints or "hardware store" paints for metal.  it would take a whole book to explain the difference.   <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||09-16-2001||10:12 PM||||The spray can primers are alright as long as they're laquer based,and all the bodywork is sealed with a catalyzed paint before the finish coats.<BR>As for the brush,uh,don't bother.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||09-17-2001||08:40 AM||||At least they didnt tell you to do it with a roller.  yuck!<P>Even with rattle can primers I wouldnt want to do a whole car with it.  The stuff takes forever to dry before you can really sand it, and with actual automotive paint you can compensate the mixture of reducer/hardener you use to compensate for the temperature and desired drying time.  I have used both acrylic enamel and lacquer primers out of a spray gun.  The acrylic sands better in my opinion, where as the lacquer drys very fast.  Either primer you use you can spray acrylic enamel over, which is most likely what your color coat and clear coat is going to be.<P>If you've never painted a car before and don't have someone to show you the correct way to do it, I might suggest taking your car to a shop if you really want it to come out looking good.||||reg||1
